Z||000004||sjp777||06-03-2001||10:32 PM||||If price doesn't matter, get a V8 and stop trying to get blood out of a stone.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_4.jpg"> <BR>88GT 5sp, in desperate need of an LT1<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||12
Z||000005||Black88GT||06-03-2001||10:42 PM||||Contrary to popular Fiero owner belief, there are other ways to get power in a Fiero besides the SBC, ways that require no kit and no extra weight.<P>You can get 200 Hp out of a 2.8 for less then the cost of a V8 Kit.  However, I would recommend starting with the 3.4 because of the improved bottom end oiling and obviously more displacement.  It would be much easier/cheaper to get 200 Hp out of a 3.4 then a 2.8.  You could get about 230 out of a 3.4 for less then a V8 kit.  Some Fiero owners put 250 Hp V8's in their cars so your not that far off.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Sanfran||06-03-2001||11:29 PM||||I just spent a small fortune on converting my 4 cyl. to a 2.8!  Not going to put a new engine in it.  The 2.8 will hold up to the 200 hp though, right.  Over the years I plan on turbo'ing it.  <P>Not worried about cash as it will be done over an extended period of time.<P>So it is possible and the engine will hold up?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1626330&a=12427866&p=45623747">  <BR>'86 2m4 <BR>Modified w/5spd. 2.8||||reg||1
Z||000007||Sanfran||06-03-2001||11:31 PM||||By "price doesn't mean anything", that's over a few years (ie. new cam, lifters, power pulley, turbo, etc.).  I'm not willing to spend a few more thousand putting a V8 in it. <P>All I want to know is how much reliable horsepower u can get out of a 2.8.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1626330&a=12427866&p=45623747">    <BR>'86 2m4 <BR>Modified w/5spd. 2.8<P>[This message has been edited by Sanfran (edited 06-03-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Sanfran (edited 06-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Black88GT||06-03-2001||11:45 PM||||You could get 200 reliable Hp out of a 2.8.  A lot more then that I would question (unless forged everything).  Basically you use a 3.1 crank and bore the block .030 over to achieve 3.2 displacement.  Then you can use your basic performance goodies like a Crane Cam, forged pistons/SBC rods (for strength), Cloyes Double Roller Timing Chain.  Then for the heads get them ported, polished, bowl blended, use stainless steel non magnetic racing valves, Hardened Bronze Guides, A.R.P. Head Stud kit, radius valve job, 1.6 Roller Rockers, chromemoly pushrods, Hi-Po Valve Springs and Retainers.<P>Use a cold Air Setup with port matched intake manifolds, Ported TB/plenum, underdrive pulleys, MSD-6 series ignition with MSD coil and 8mm or thicker wires.<P>Think thats it, you should definitely have 200 reliable Hp.<P>&lt;edited for typo's&gt;<p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 06-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Sanfran||06-04-2001||12:22 AM||||Don't forget the Turbo (maybe twin)!  Another thing is, is that we are going to strengthen the frame and put on some sway bars and lower it a little bit, and some good breaks; so that when it has 200 hp it'll handle great.<P>Thanks, I'll print this off and save it for later.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1626330&a=12427866&p=45623747">  <BR>'86 2m4 <BR>Modified w/5spd. 2.8||||reg||1
Z||000010||Ken Wittlief||06-04-2001||09:29 AM||||When I was a mechanic in the Coast Guard, the one thing that I learned that surprized me the most, is that a turbo is the simplist way, and often the best way, to increase the horsepower of any internal combustion engine. The second is to add a blower.<P>   The thing about this that surprized me is, at the time (back in the late 70s') almost all diesel powered trucks and machinery had turbos or blowers. It was standard. When they need HP they pump more air into the engine - plain and simple.<P>  The other thing back then that stood out was that all diesel equipment used all stainless steel exhaust - that is getting to be more and more standard on cars now, but back then I think only Porche had stainless exhaust as standard equipment.<P>   You want more HP - you need more air and fuel going through the motor. You can either do it by increasing the displacement - bigger engine - and letting it suck the air in (and live with the bigger/heavier motor), or by cramming the air and fuel into the smaller engine (or by using a more powerful fuel, like N/O).<P>  or put a gas turbine in your car, like the original BatMobile (c:||||reg||1
Z||000011||Sanfran||06-04-2001||01:59 PM||||Thanks, for the info.<BR>Planning on Turbo'ing it,as I had my new sail panel windows with the "Turbo" decal on.<BR>One of the mods. I have are the Holley scoop and K&N air filter.  So I have more than the normal amount of air coming into the engine<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1626330&a=12427866&p=45623747">  <BR>'86 2m4 <BR>Modified w/5spd. 2.8||||reg||1
Z||000012||1FST2M6||06-04-2001||02:28 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstdyno_manymods.jpg"> <P><BR>basicly stock motor... just bolt ons and electronics... 154hp 168tq.... i'm sure with a decent cam and real head work you'd be stomping some MAJOR nuts. <P>oh it's an automatic too... <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig1fst.jpg">      <BR>www.engineered.net<BR>www.epdyno.com<BR>www.enthuzacar.com||||reg||1
Z||000013||Sanfran||06-04-2001||02:40 PM||||Another thing,<BR>Does anyone have a 5spd 2.8?<BR>When is the best time to shift to get the most out of each gear?<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1626330&a=12427866&p=45623747">  <BR>'86 2m4 <BR>Modified w/5spd. 2.8||||reg||1
Z||000014||thomas_l||06-04-2001||03:14 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Sanfran:<BR><B>Another thing,<BR>Does anyone have a 5spd 2.8?<BR>When is the best time to shift to get the most out of each gear?<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Mine is a 4 speed and after 4,500 it starts to wheeze. Take a close look at that dyno run graph and you can see why. I have been known to hit the 6k rev limiter in 1st and 2nd while autocrossing tho   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by thomas_l (edited 06-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||Raydar||06-04-2001||03:57 PM||||There's a guy in our club that has a 3.4 that is based on a 2.8 block. It was overbored and stroked. I believe it has a 3.1 crank. Also has a bunch of other goodies.<BR>200 HP? His is probably close.<BR>You can't do this with every 2.8, though. It depends upon the individual casting, whether the cylinder walls will be thick enough to stand that much of an overbore.<BR>This is the same thing that ARI is doing. <A HREF="http://www.engine-parts.com/GMV6/gm34da.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.engine-parts.com/GMV6/gm34da.html</A> <BR>This may not be what you're looking for, but it is an option. <BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"||||reg||1
Z||000016||rogergarrison||06-04-2001||05:15 PM||||I have 3.1, which is for all intents  and purposes , is a 2.8. I got a turbo, and when it was first done 3 years ago, it dynoed at just a shade over 270 hp. Im sure its lost some uuumpp in 80,000 miles though.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1234884&a=9116379&p=34556781"> ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Toddster||06-04-2001||05:56 PM||||Hi sanfran,<P>Upping the horses in the 2.8 is a great solution, but like someone else pointed-out, it can be pricy. also, there are not too many people who can do it well. I can recommend Loyals Automotive in Hayward, CA. Ask for Rick.<P>$2500 and a core will get you a fully built long block with balanced and blueprinted internals, hyperutetic pistons (.30 over), roller rockers, comp springs, erson cam, very very light head work (all that is needed), port matching, performance oil pump, etc. After that you need to build-up the top end. I recommend 3.1 injectors, a Corvette adjustable Fuel pressure regulator (set at around 47psi), Accel or MSD coil, 8.8 mil wires, split fire plugs (NO PLATINUM!!!), Accel rotor and cap, port your exhaust manifolds!, K&N filter, 4-core radiator, and replace all sensors.<P>Good for 200hp! add a turbo and be sure to wear a diaper next time you step on the gas.||||reg||10
Z||000018||Sanfran||06-04-2001||11:07 PM||||Living in Toronto, Canada I don't think I'll be sending it to Cali; I'm sure I could find a guy here to do it.  It all sounds good though.||||reg||1
